   "comments": [
    {
     "author": {
      "accountId": "5bba58f3eb6f6e2ccfd097c6",
      "accountType": "atlassian",
      "active": true,
      "avatarUrls": {
       "16x16": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0f212807d7bbe91a49a7c4620c5bfdac?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FBP-1.png",
       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0f212807d7bbe91a49a7c4620c5bfdac?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FBP-1.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0f212807d7bbe91a49a7c4620c5bfdac?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FBP-1.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0f212807d7bbe91a49a7c4620c5bfdac?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FBP-1.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Ben Pennell",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=5bba58f3eb6f6e2ccfd097c6",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": {
      "content": [
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "I think the jacoco setup already in fcrepo is close to what we would need, but I believe it is building for sonar. I'm not sure if they have an open source free tier for github integration.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "In terms of other options, UNC currently uses ",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "marks": [
           {
            "attrs": {
             "href": "https://codeclimate.com/quality/pricing"
            },
            "type": "link"
           }
          ],
          "text": "CodeClimate",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "text": " which does code linting and presents coverage. There are also ",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "marks": [
           {
            "attrs": {
             "href": "https://about.codecov.io/pricing/"
            },
            "type": "link"
           }
          ],
          "text": "CodeCov",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "text": " and ",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "marks": [
           {
            "attrs": {
             "href": "https://coveralls.io/pricing"
            },
            "type": "link"
           }
          ],
          "text": "Coveralls",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "text": ", which both seem to have open source free tiers and github action integration. ",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "I am most familiar with codeclimate. It adds over all test coverage to the PR and can be configured to fail a PR if test coverage is below a threshold. But it requires a browser plugin to display code coverage per file/line in the PR view. It can be a bit heavy handed with the linting, although it’s highly configurable.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "It seems like Codecov directly integrates with the PR view without any additional steps, and shows coverage changes. It does not do any linting, which could be a plus or minus depending on how annoying you find your build failing because of code quality issues.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "Coverall produces a report which gets linked to in a comment in the PR, but doesn't directly integrate with the github file view.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "I believe someone that is the owner or admin to the fcrepo github repo would have to be the one to enable any of these tools. I don't have a strong opinion about which to use, although my impression is codecov would require less adjustment/setup than codeclimate.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       }
      ],
      "type": "doc",
      "version": 1
     },
