     "body": "<p>I think the jacoco setup already in fcrepo is close to what we would need, but I believe it is building for sonar. I'm not sure if they have an open source free tier for github integration.</p>\n\n<p>In terms of other options, UNC currently uses <a href=\"https://codeclimate.com/quality/pricing\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">CodeClimate</a> which does code linting and presents coverage. There are also <a href=\"https://about.codecov.io/pricing/\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">CodeCov</a> and <a href=\"https://coveralls.io/pricing\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Coveralls</a>, which both seem to have open source free tiers and github action integration. </p>\n\n<p>I am most familiar with codeclimate. It adds over all test coverage to the PR and can be configured to fail a PR if test coverage is below a threshold. But it requires a browser plugin to display code coverage per file/line in the PR view. It can be a bit heavy handed with the linting, although it’s highly configurable.</p>\n\n<p>It seems like Codecov directly integrates with the PR view without any additional steps, and shows coverage changes. It does not do any linting, which could be a plus or minus depending on how annoying you find your build failing because of code quality issues.</p>\n\n<p>Coverall produces a report which gets linked to in a comment in the PR, but doesn't directly integrate with the github file view.</p>\n\n<p>I believe someone that is the owner or admin to the fcrepo github repo would have to be the one to enable any of these tools. I don't have a strong opinion about which to use, although my impression is codecov would require less adjustment/setup than codeclimate.</p>",
     "created": "10/Jan/25 3:41 PM",
